                "A free-running loop keeps its state implicitly in a growing message list, so you can't easily say where it is, resume it after a crash, or stop it before a risky step. Production agents make state explicit: a typed state object (the task, the plan, what's done, intermediate results, budget used) and a defined set of steps with transitions between them.",
                "Graph frameworks such as LangGraph model this directly: nodes are steps (an LLM call, a tool, a check), edges are transitions (fixed or decided by the model), and a checkpointer saves the state after every step. That gives you resumption after failures, time-travel debugging (replay from any step), and interrupts: pause before a node, show a human the proposed action, continue with their decision.",
                "Long-running or business-critical agents add durable execution (workflow engines such as Temporal, or the framework's own persistence): every step is recorded, retried on transient failure and never repeated after success. Combined with idempotent tools, this is what stops a retried step from charging a card twice.",
                "Human-in-the-loop is a design decision, not an afterthought. Decide which actions need approval (irreversible, external, expensive, or low-confidence), what the reviewer sees (the proposed action and why), and what happens on rejection. HumanLayer's '12-Factor Agents' puts it simply: own your control flow, own your context window, and make the agent a stateless reducer over explicit state you control.",
